    "features": 40,
    "bio": "Clint Eastwood is American cinema's great economist — a director who spent five decades stripping away everything a film doesn't need. From Play Misty for Me (1971) to Juror #2 (2024), he directed forty features at a working rhythm nobody else in Hollywood could sustain: shot fast, shot lean, first takes kept, actors trusted. The persona he built in front of the camera — the loner who solves problems with violence — became precisely the myth his best films took apart; Unforgiven, A Perfect World and Mystic River are all reckonings with what violence actually costs the people who deal in it. His late period turned to institutions and the ordinary people caught inside them, told with the same refusal to decorate. He was never a stylist and never wanted to be — the style is the absence of style, and at its best it feels less like direction than like judgment quietly withheld.",
